\maketitle

The operational semantics of the programming language defines a transition system on execution graphs.
Instead of a detailed syntax, we assume that there is a function $\nextop_P(G)$ which takes
an execution graph and returns a 
set of possible next events from the program $P$: these are the next operations
of the unblocked threads, after the events in $G$ have transpired.
If $\nextop_P(G)$ is empty, then there are no remaining operations and the program has terminated. 


We define a labeled transition system ($\lts$) $\mathcal{T}(P)$ on the set of sequentially consistent execution graphs.  
First, for any execution graph $G$ and location $l$, we define $G.\momax{l}$ as the unique maximal event $e \in G.\opw_l$ in the $G.\mo{l}$ ordering.
Intuitively, $G.\momax{l}$ is the last write to the location $l$ in $G$. 
The labeled transition system $\mathcal{T}(P) = (S, S_0, \xrightarrow{})$ is defined as:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $S$ is the set of sequentially consistent execution graphs.
    \item $S_0$ is the execution graph $G_{\init} = (\set{\init}, \emptyset, \emptyset)$ consisting of the singleton node $\init$.
    \item $\xrightarrow{} = \bigcup_{t \in \Tid}\{\}\xrightarrow{t}\}$, where $\xrightarrow{t}$ denotes the local transition relation of thread $t \in \Tid$.  
\end{enumerate}
There is an edge $G_1 \xrightarrow{t} G_2$ if
there is an $e\in \nextop_P(G_1)$ with $t = \tid(e)$, and
\begin{enumerate} 
\item $G_2.E = G_1.E \cup \set{e}$.


\item $G_2.\rf{} = \begin{cases} 
G_1.\rf{} & \op(e.\ell) = \opw \\ 
G_1.\rf{} \cup \{(G_1.\momax{\loc(e)}, e)\} & \text{otherwise} 
\end{cases}$
\item $G_2.\mo{}{} = \begin{cases} 
G_1.\mo{}{} & \op(e.\ell) = \opr \\ 
G_1.\mo{} \cup \{(e', e) \mid e' \in G_1.W_{\loc(e)}\} & \text{otherwise} 
\end{cases}$


\end{enumerate}

An execution graph is \emph{reachable} if $G_{\init} \xrightarrow{\cdot}^* G$.
An execution graph is \emph{maximal} if it has no successors,
i.e., $\nextop_P(G) = \emptyset$.
We write $C(P)$ for the number of maximal reachable execution graphs in $\mathcal{T}(P)$.





Note that $\mathcal{T}(P)$ is a DAG but not necessarily a tree: two 
different paths in the exploration can reach the same execution graph.
For example, if two threads each read a variable, the two possible thread schedules both lead to the same execution graph.

\begin{comment}
For given execution graphs $G$ and $G'$, a thread $t$, a label $l$, and a write event $w\in E_{G}$ such that $\floc(\flab(w))=\floc(l)$, we use $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ to denote the relation defined as \begin{enumerate}
    \item $E_{G'}=E_G\cup\{e\}$ with $\ftid(e)=t$ and $\flab(e)=l$;
    \item $\po{G'}=\po{G}\cup\{(\po{G}^{max}(t),e)\}$;
    \item $\mo{G'}=\mo{G}$ if $\op(l)=\opr$ and $\mo{G'}=\mo{G}\cup\{(w,e)\}$ if $\op(l)=\opw$; and
    \item $\rf{G'}=\rf{G}$ if $\op(l)=\opw$ and $\rf{G'}=\rf{G}\cup\{(w,e)\}$ if $\op(l)=\opr$ and $\fval(l)=\fval(\lab(e))$.

\end{enumerate}
We denote the special relation when $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ and $w=\mo{G}^{max}(\floc(l))$ as $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G'$  which reflects the allowed memory accesses under sequential consistency (SC). Let the special execution graph $(\init{},\emptyset,\emptyset,\emptyset)$ be denoted as $G_0$. We say that a execution graph $G$ is reachable from $G_0$ if either $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G$ for some tuple $(t,l,w)$ or $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ and $G$ is reachable from $G'$. Similarly, we say that a execution graph $G$ is reachable from $G_0$ under SC if either $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G$ for some tuple $(t,l,w)$ or $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G'$ and $G$ is reachable from $G'$ under SC. We use $\egraph$ to denote the set of all execution graphs reachable from $G_0$ and $\scg$ to denote the set of all execution graphs reachable from $G_0$ under SC. 
\end{comment}


\begin{comment}

\textbf{Events}.
An event \( e \in E_{\rho} \) of a concurrent program $\rho$ is a pair \( ( t,\,  l) \), where $t$ denotes the unique thread identifier and $l\in \lab$ is a label of the event is the label associated with the event, as defined above. Let $\ftid$  and $\flab$ denote the obvious functions that maps each event to its thread identifier and labels. We use $\opw_x$ and $\opr_x$ to denote the set of all write and read events on the memory location $x$. The initial set of events \( \init{}\) is defined as \( \{( \bot, (\opw,x,0) ) \mid x \in \loc\}\). We remark that events access local registers have label $\epsilon$.

\textbf{Execution Graphs} To define the semantic of the concurrent program, we use execution graphs to model concurrent accesses on shared memory locations. 
Formally, an execution graph $G$ is a tuple $(E_G,\rf{G},\mo{G},\po{G})$ where:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  $E_G$ is a set of events. 
    \item $\po{G}$ is the "program order" that totally orders events from the same thread.  We use $\po{G}^{max}(t)$ to denote the maximal event of thread $t$.
    \item $\mathsf{rf_G}$ is the "read from" relation such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item if $(e_1,e_2)\in \mathsf{rf_G}$ then there exists some $x\in \loc$ such that $\mathsf{lab}(e_1)=(\opw,x)$ and $\mathsf{lab}(e_2)=(\opr,x)$.
        \item if $(e_1,e_2)\in \mathsf{rf_G}$ and $(e_3,e_2)\in \mathsf{rf_G}$, then $e_1=e_3$.
        \item for every $e\in E_\mathsf{G}$ that is a read event, there exists an $e'$ such that $(e',e)\in \mathsf{rf_G}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    \item $\mathsf{mo_G}$ is the modification order which totally orders the writes on the same memory location. We use $\mo{G}^{max}(x)$ to denote the latest write on the memory location $x$.
\end{enumerate}
For given execution graphs $G$ and $G'$, a thread $t$, a label $l$, and a write event $w\in E_{G}$ such that $\floc(\flab(w))=\floc(l)$, we use $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ to denote the relation defined as \begin{enumerate}
    \item $E_{G'}=E_G\cup\{e\}$ with $\ftid(e)=t$ and $\flab(e)=l$;
    \item $\po{G'}=\po{G}\cup\{(\po{G}^{max}(t),e)\}$;
    \item $\mo{G'}=\mo{G}$ if $\op(l)=\opr$ and $\mo{G'}=\mo{G}\cup\{(w,e)\}$ if $\op(l)=\opw$; and
    \item $\rf{G'}=\rf{G}$ if $\op(l)=\opw$ and $\rf{G'}=\rf{G}\cup\{(w,e)\}$ if $\op(l)=\opr$ and $\fval(l)=\fval(\lab(e))$.

\end{enumerate}
We denote the special relation when $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ and $w=\mo{G}^{max}(\floc(l))$ as $G\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G'$  which reflects the allowed memory accesses under sequential consistency (SC). Let the special execution graph $(\init{},\emptyset,\emptyset,\emptyset)$ be denoted as $G_0$. We say that a execution graph $G$ is reachable from $G_0$ if either $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G$ for some tuple $(t,l,w)$ or $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}G'$ and $G$ is reachable from $G'$. Similarly, we say that a execution graph $G$ is reachable from $G_0$ under SC if either $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G$ for some tuple $(t,l,w)$ or $G_0\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC}G'$ and $G$ is reachable from $G'$ under SC. We use $\egraph$ to denote the set of all execution graphs reachable from $G_0$ and $\scg$ to denote the set of all execution graphs reachable from $G_0$ under SC. 
\end{comment}

\begin{comment}





\textbf{Bounded concurrent programming languages}. Let $\loc$, $\reg$, and $\val$ be finite sets of locations, registers and values. 
Every expression $e$ is constructed with values from $\val$ and values stored in local registers. The instruction $r:=e$ assigns the value of $e$ into local register $r$; $x:=e$ stores the value of $x$ in location $x$ and similarly $r:=x$ reads the value from $x$ and stores it into register $r$; if $e$ goto $n$ instruction jumps to line $n$ of the program if $e$ is true. 
A sequential program $S\in \mathsf{SProg}$ is a finite map from natural numbers to instructions and a concurrent program $P$ is a parallel composition of sequential programs. The size of a sequential program $S$, denoted as $|S|$ is the number of  natural numbers that are mapped to instructions in $S$. Similarly, the size of a concurrent program $P$, denoted as $|P|$ is defined as $\sum_{t\in\tid}P(t)$. We say that $P$ is bounded by $k\leq |P|$ if every sequential consistent execution of $P$ is bounded by $k$.

\textbf{Labeled Transition System}. The semantic of a sequential program can be given by a labeled transition system. 

\begin{definition}
    A labeled transition system (LTS) $M$ over an alphabet $\Sigma$ is a tuple $(Q, q_0,\delta)$ where $Q$ is the set of possibly infinite states, $q_0$ is the unique initial state, and $\delta:Q\times\Sigma\rightarrow Q$ is the transition function. 
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}
    A label $l$ in $\lab\subseteq \{\opw,\opr\}\times\loc\times\val$ is represented as a tuple $(\mathsf{o},x,v)$ where $\mathsf{o},x,v$ denote the operation type, location, and value of the label, respectively. The functions $\op$, $\floc$, and $\fval$  return the operation type, location and value of a given label.
\end{definition}
The transition system $M_\rho$ induced by a sequential program $\rho\in \text{SProg}$ over $\Sigma=\lab\cup \{\epsilon\}$ is defined as follows: 
 the states ${M_\rho}.Q$ are pairs of the form $(i,\gamma) $ where $i$ is the program counter that marks the position of the instruction and $\gamma$ is the store that maps every local register $r\in\reg$(or simply an expression $e$) to a value $v\in \val$. The initial state ${M_\rho}.q_0$ is $(0,\lambda r.0)$ The transition function ${M_\rho}.\delta$ is defined as: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item ${M_\rho}.\delta((i,\gamma), \epsilon)=(i+1,\gamma')$ if $\rho(i)=r:=e$ and $\gamma'=\gamma[r\leftarrow e]$.
    \item ${M_\rho}.\delta((i,\gamma), \epsilon)=(n,\gamma)$ if $\rho(i)=\text{if } e \text{ goto } n$.
    \item ${M_\rho}.\delta((i,\gamma), (\opr,x,v)))=(i+1,\gamma')$ if $\rho(i)=r:=x$ and $\gamma'=\gamma[r\leftarrow v]$.
    \item ${M_\rho}.\delta((i,\gamma), (\opw,x,\gamma(e)))=(i+1,\gamma)$ if $\rho(i)=x:=e$.
\end{enumerate}
Similarly, the transition system $M_P$ induced by a concurrent program $P$ over alphabet $\tid\times (\lab\cup \{\epsilon\})$ is defined as: the states $M_P.Q$ are 
tuples in $\prod_{t\in\tid}M_{P(t)}.Q$; the initial state $M_P.q_0$ is the tuple $(M_{P(t_1)}.q_0,M_{P(t_2)}.q_0\dots M_{P(t_k)}.q_0)$ for $\tid=\{t_1,t_2,\dots t_k\}$. The transition function $M_P.\delta$ is given as:
$M_P.\delta((q_1,q_2 \dots q_k),(t_i,l))=(q_1',q_2'\dots q_k')$ if $M_{P(t_i)}.\delta(q_i,l)=q_i'$ and for all $t_j\in \tid\setminus\{t_i\}$, $q_j=q_j'$.



\textbf{Concurrent System under Sequential Consistency}
A concurrent system consists of a concurrent programming language $P$ and a memory model. For our purpose, we use $(\scg,\overset{t,l,w}{\rightarrow}_{SC})$ as the memory model under the assumption of sequential consistency. A LTS $M_P^{SC}$ induced by a concurrent system is over alphabet $\tid\times (\lab\cup \{\epsilon\})$. Its states are pairs from $M_P\times \scg$ and the initial state is $(M_P.q_0,G_0)$.
The transition function $M_P^{SC}.\delta$ is given as:
\[
    M_P^{SC}.\delta((M_{P}.q, G),(t,l))=(M_{P}.q',G') \text{ if } M_P.\delta(q,(t,l))=M_{P}.q' \text{ and } G\overset{t,l,\mo{G}^{max}(\floc(l))}{\longrightarrow}_{SC}G'\ 
\]
We also denote $ M_P^{SC}.\delta(q,l)=q'$ as $q\overset{l}{\to}_{M_P^{SC}}q'$
 It is worth mentioning that with our definition, for any two states $(q_1,G_1)$ and $(q_2,G_2)$ of $M_P^{SC}.Q$, we have $G_1=G_2\rightarrow  q_1=q_2$.
 A state $(q,G)\in {M_P^{SC}}$ is a final states if there is no valid transitions from $(q,G)$ to any state and $G$ is said to be a complete execution graph. 
 In particular, we say that $P$ admits an execution graph $G$ if $(q,G)$ is a reachable state of $P$ for some control state $q$.  

\end{comment}

\begin{comment}
    a. Concurrent programs syntax and semantics
    b. Equivalence on executions (sound equivalences preserve control and data flow)
    c. Estimation problem - given a program P and an equivalence, count the number of equivalence classes in the set P_n, where P_n represents the set of executions of P whose length is n.
    d. Monte carlo estimation without equivalence (Rupak's old paper)
\end{comment}

We now present our first estimator, which works \textit{as long as} $\mathcal{T}(P)$ \textit{is a tree}. It is based on Monte Carlo estimation.
The algorithm, given below, was described by Knuth \cite{knuth75}.

\begin{verse}
\label{algo:K}
\textbf{Algorithm K} \cite{knuth75}. 
Given a tree $T$, run the following randomized procedure.
Starting at the root,
for each node $n_i$ with $d_i$ children, choose one of its $d_i$ children uniformly at random and move there, until reaching a leaf $n_k$.
Stop and output $d_1 \cdot d_2 \cdot \ldots \cdot d_{k-1}$ as an estimate of the number of leaves of $T$.
\end{verse}

\begin{proposition}{\cite{knuth75}}
If $\mathcal{T}(P)$ is a tree, \textbf{Algorithm K} is a poly-time unbiased estimator for $C(P)$.
\end{proposition}

We observe that each path $n_1 \ldots n_k$ of the tree from its root $n_1$ to a leaf $n_k$ is reached by the random walk with probability $p(n_1,\dots,n_k) = 1/(d_1d_2\ldots d_{k-1})$. Since the output of our algorithm for the path $n_1,\dots,n_k$
is exactly $1/p(n_1,\dots,n_k)$, it follows that the expected value of our output
is simply the number of all possible paths $n_1,\dots,n_k$,
which is exactly the number of leaves.


\begin{tcolorbox}[
    colback=SkyBlue!5,
    colframe=blue!20,
    boxrule=1pt,
    arc=2pt,
    width=\textwidth,
    breakable
]

\begin{example}
Consider the program
\begin{equation*}
  \tag{\textsc{r+w+w}}
  \label{ex:r+w+w}
  \inarr{ \textcolor{Red}{\readInst{b}{x}} }{ \textcolor{blue}{\writeInst{x}{1}} }{ \textcolor{Dandelion!60!black}{\writeInst{x}{2}} }
\end{equation*}
\cref{fig:r+w+w-t} shows the transition system $\mathcal{T}(\mathsf{R+W+W})$.
The estimate for any random path through the tree is $3\times 2\times 1 = 6$, and the expectation is also 6. 
\qed
\end{example}


