\begin{comment}

The \trust algorithm is the state-of-the-art stateless model model checking algorithm that achieves optimality while using only polynomial space with respect to the size of the program. 
We briefly outline how \trust operates. 
Conceptually, \trust starts from the initial execution graph and incrementally explores all possible consistent extensions permitted by the memory model. Each constructed graph represents an equivalence class of executions. At each step, the algorithm  schedules one of the enabled threads and recursively explores all possible reorderings of events in a depth-first manner. The exploration terminates once all equivalence classes have been enumerated.

An execution graph initially contains only the \texttt{init} event and is expanded incrementally by adding an event that represents the next operation interpreted from a scheduled enabled thread. When a read event is added, \trust expands the search space by forward visiting different consistent RF relationships relative to existing writes on the same memory location. When a write event is added, \trust expands the search space in two ways: (1) similarly to the case of read events, it forward revisits different consistent MO relationships relative to existing write events, and (2) it also backward revisits earlier reads in restricted execution graphs—constructed by removing all events that have been added after the read and are not in the PORF-prefix of the recently added write—to account for cases where those reads could now consistently read from the new write in an optimal manner. This means the algorithm does not produce any two equivalent restricted execution graphs after processing any backward revisit.
How \trust decides when to perform a revisit is critical to its guarantee of optimality. Blindly revisiting earlier reads for every newly added write can result in redundant exploration of the same execution graph, thereby violating the algorithm’s optimality guarantee. On the other hand, naively remembering all revisits performed so far to avoid repetition requires exponential memory. To address this, \trust relies on a novel condition called the maximal extension condition, which determines precisely when a revisit is necessary—purely based on the current execution graph, rather than the full program state or previously visited configurations. The maximal extension condition ensures that if two execution graphs differ only in the set of events that would later be removed through backward revisits, then the revisit is performed on exactly one of them. This enables \trust to achieve both completeness and optimality while remaining within polynomial space. Notably, the recursion depth of \trust is bounded by $O(k^2)$, where $k$ is the bound on the concurrent program. 









\begin{example}
\label{example1}
   Consider a simple concurrent program with three threads: $t_1$, $t_2$, and $t_3$, where $t_1$ reads from a shared variable $x$, and both $t_2$ and $t_3$ write to $x$. Suppose we fix a schedule where $t_1$ executes first, followed by $t_2$ and then $t_3$. \trust begins by exploring the execution graph in which the read from $t_1$ observes the initial value of $x$ (denoted $\mathsf{init}$), corresponding to graph G1 in Figure~\ref{exec-graphs}.Next, when the write from $t_2$ is scheduled, \trust first considers the case where the write is ordered after the read via a from-read ($\mathsf{fr}$) edge, yielding graph G2. It then explores a backward revisit that allows the read to instead read from this write, resulting in graph G7.From G2, \trust proceeds to schedule the write from $t_3$ and explores two possible modification orders: one where the write from $t_3$ follows the write from $t_2$ (G3), and one where it precedes it (G4). It then explores additional graphs where the read reads from the newly scheduled write (G5 and G6), depending on the modification order and visibility of writes. From G7, \trust explores the two possible modification orders introduced by scheduling the write from $t_3$, resulting in graphs G8 and G9.It is important to notice that from G7 \trust does not explore the possibility when the read can read from the write from $t_3$. This is because G7 does not satisfy the maximal extension condition.  
\qed
\end{example}





















\end{comment}

The state estimation problem was also considered in \cite{gator}, where the authors give a randomized procedure based on the \trust algorithm.
However, as we show below, that estimator is not an unbiased estimator; thus, even with exponentially many samples,
it has no guarantees to converge to the correct answer.
Experimentally, the estimator converges to incorrect values quite frequently.







The procedure is also based on the \trust algorithm, and proceeds as follows.
First, it preferentially schedules writes over reads.
For each read or write event, it explores one of the forward-revisits uniformly at random. 
Instead of computing the estimate on the fly, it records the number of values each read can read from. 
In case a new write is added and \trust detects that a backward revisit would be required, the estimator
does not perform the backward revisit but increments the map entry for any read that could read from this new value by one. 
At the end, it takes the product of the ``possible reads-from'' counts for every read and the ``possible modification order" 
for every write to produce the final estimate.

Intuitively, the estimator assumes one can generate all graphs in $\mathcal{T}(P)$ 
without any backward revisits, simply by scheduling write events before read events.

When this assumption holds, the estimate is unbiased. 
However, the assumption may not hold. 
Since the algorithm forbids backward revisits, it may omit graphs 
reachable only via backward revisits and produce a biased estimate.



